1
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:42,000
A little pig goes a long way.

2
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:18,000
Babe is a fantasy, drama, comedy film directed by Chris Noonan.

3
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:54,000
Babe is a little pig who doesn't quite know his place in the world.

4
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:30,000
With a bunch of odd friends, like Ferdinand the duck who thinks he is a rooster and Fly the dog he calls mum, Babe
realises that he has the makings to become the greatest sheep pig of all time, and Farmer Hoggett knows it.

5
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:06,000
With the help of the sheep dogs, Babe learns that a pig
can be anything that he wants to be.

6
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:42,000
Babe (voice), played by Christine Cavanaugh.

7
00:32:12,000 --> 00:32:18,000
Fly (voice), played by Miriam Margolyes.

8
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:54,000
Ferdinand (voice), played by Danny Mann.

9
00:41:24,000 --> 00:41:30,000
Rex (voice), played by Hugo Weaving.

10
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:06,000
Maa (voice), played by Miriam Flynn.

11
00:50:36,000 --> 00:50:42,000
Farmer Hoggett, played by James Cromwell.

12
00:55:12,000 --> 00:55:18,000
Babe is a 1995 comedy-drama film directed by Chris
Noonan, who co-wrote it with George Miller.

13
00:59:48,000 --> 00:59:54,000
It is an adaptation of Dick King-Smith's 1983 novel The Sheep-Pig, which tells
the story of a farm pig who wants to do the work of a sheepdog.

14
01:04:24,000 --> 01:04:30,000
The film is narrated by Roscoe Lee Browne and the main animal characters
are played by both real animals and animatronic puppets.

15
01:09:00,000 --> 01:09:06,000
Babe was filmed in Robertson, New South Wales, Australia, in 1994 and released theatrically in the United States and Canada on 4 August 1995,
going on to become a critical and commercial success with seven Academy Award nominations, including Best Director and Best Picture.

16
01:13:36,000 --> 01:13:42,000
A sequel, Babe: Pig in the City, was released in 1998, with most
of the starring actors reprising their roles.

17
01:18:12,000 --> 01:18:18,000
However, it did not perform as well commercially or critically as the original.

18
01:22:48,000 --> 01:22:54,000
The film explores themes of farm, sheep, pig, alarm clock, cat.

19
01:27:24,000 --> 01:27:30,000
The tone is lighthearted and emotional.
